[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Its reports describe two
problems. The subjects look alike, so the desk merged them on subject
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). `causeOf` in `causes.ts` ties each
report to a cause, and it has two: `parser` and `hint`. Each cause has its own ticket.

The desk closed #4471 on 5 October. Its update ends both: `parser` is fixed
(`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`), and `hint` won't be fixed, because the booking-v3
form is retired.

<CauseTickets ticket={4471} filed={tickets} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Some `parser` reports read as a one-day move, not a day/month swap ("Tuesday 12 May, calendar
says Monday 11 May"). `causeOf` still ties them to `parser`, so they are counted there, not on a
ticket of their own. A ticket for them would re-claim reports that already belong to `parser`.
</Callout>

## How to tell them apart

Read the body, not the subject or the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser; the
cause depends on the tenant, its booking form and the channel (`causes.ts`).

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file writes
  the day first, so the day and month swap ("7 April went out on 4 July"). Comes in by app, email,
  or a `booking-v2` form. The one-day moves sit here too: `causeOf` ties them to `parser`.
- **`hint`**: the same swap, but only through the booking form of a `booking-v3` tenant. That
  form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, people type what it says, and the form then
  reads the date day first. Fixing the parser did not change the hint; retiring the form did.

A few bodies name no dates ("sent my mother to the dentist on the wrong day"). They are counted
by tenant and channel, like the rest.

